<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Landgraviate of Hesse-Marburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1567 CE – 1604 CE · capital Marburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Hessian landgraviate centred on the university town of Marburg, whose division in 1604 set off decades of conflict between Kassel and Darmstadt.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The 1567 division of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/landgraviate-of-hesse">Landgraviate of Hesse</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-landgrave-of-hesse-marburg">Louis IV</a>, second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-landgrave-of-hesse">Philip the Magnanimous</a>, about a quarter of the land, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a> and its university, founded by his father in 1527 as the first Protestant university.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-landgrave-of-hesse-marburg">Louis IV</a> was a strict Lutheran who defended orthodoxy against the Calvinist leanings of his brother in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>. His land was largely agrarian, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg's</a> castle and university at its centre. He married twice but had no children, and his will required that his heirs keep the Lutheran faith.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-landgrave-of-hesse-marburg">Louis IV</a> died in 1604, his lands were divided between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hesse">Hesse-Kassel</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Hesse-Darmstadt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-landgrave-of-hesse-kassel">Maurice of Hesse-Kassel</a>'s introduction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a> broke the will's terms and started a long dispute that culminated in the Hessian War of 1645 to 1648.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1567 CE – 1604 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:90.89%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1567 CE – 1604 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Marburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Louis IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hessen-Marburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1586&amp;focus=landgraviate-of-hesse-marburg" aria-label="Landgraviate of Hesse-Marburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4880 415 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Landgraviate of Hesse-Marburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5200 671L5215 656"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5215 656h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5200 671h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5200 671h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Marburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1586&amp;focus=landgraviate-of-hesse-marburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
